#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054416 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bf25f86-6b27-5eaa-b1a1-1104478b290a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/id #uuid "59d2e907-e788-5a22-93a9-c49ff94df6ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-190" :question/problem "Given $x^2 + y^2 = \\sin{y}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$, assuming that $y = y(x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} + y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin y) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin y) \\\\\n2x + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\cos y\\frac{dy}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\cos y\\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n2y\\frac{dy}{dx} - \\cos y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2x \\\\\n(2y - \\cos y)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2x \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\frac{2x}{2y - \\cos y} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{2x}{\\cos y - 2y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054417 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2311a895-587e-5609-8a9f-9963b8cae372" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "8a6eb51f-6232-5a08-acf1-136f0d14a4d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-191" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ for the curve $xy+y^2=2x^2+4$." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y = y(x)$.\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy + y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{2} + 4) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) + \\frac{d}{dx}(y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{2} + 4) \\\\\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y\\frac{d}{dx}(x) + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 4x \\\\\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 4x \\\\\n(x + 2y)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 4x - y \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{4x - y}{x + 2y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} m &=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\left(\\dfrac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{4\\cdot 1-2}{1+2\\cdot 2} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{2}{5} \\,. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfdb93d1-d550-5939-8a47-99bd1d863a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Intercept of a Tangent or Normal Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/id #uuid "cd630daa-4813-5ca8-b3d4-5fe35c4af4fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-192" :question/problem "The equation $x^2-2x+y^2+4y=5$ represents a circle. The normal line at the point $\\left(0, -5 \\right)$ on the circle intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P$. What are the coordinates of $P$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/57/Source/Images/7c8c9a815718ae4d5afe44e65592f4b8.png)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(0,-5)$. Remember that the slope of the normal line, $m'$, is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm' = -\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y = y(x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2-2x+ y^2+4y) &=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5) \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2)-\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (2x)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (y^2)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (4y)&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5) \\\\[5pt] 2x-2+2y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}+4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=0 \\\\[5pt] (2y+4)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=2-2x \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} &=\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} m &=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\left(\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{2-2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot (-5)+4} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{2}{-6} \\\\[5pt] &=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\,. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=3\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the normal at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y-y_1 &=m'\\left(x-x_1 \\right) \\\\[5pt] y+5 &=3\\left(x-0 \\right) \\\\[5pt] y&=3x-5 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0$, so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y&=3x-5 \\\\[5pt] 0&=3x-5 \\\\[5pt] x&=\\dfrac{5}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3},\\,0 \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054485 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "af328f52-53a1-5cc1-a542-6cad8eea4c44" :question/math-academy-id "e-6114" :question/problem "Given $xy+y^2=x^2$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$, assuming that $y = y(x)$.\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy + y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) + \\frac{d}{dx}(y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\\\\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y\\frac{d}{dx}(x) + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2x \\\\\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2x \\\\\n(x + 2y)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2x - y \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{2x - y}{x + 2y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2311a895-587e-5609-8a9f-9963b8cae372" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb39b8d2-a7f1-533a-b4f7-2be722af37dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053323} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f8240d-c020-544d-bf78-3d186e783af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053324} {:answer/id #uuid "331307e8-2170-55f8-936d-6b74170340c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053325} {:answer/id #uuid "7301ee39-e742-5f38-bbc5-fbb71d864d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053326} {:answer/id #uuid "7a641cad-eaa0-57c7-9f10-70981e15c329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a641cad-eaa0-57c7-9f10-70981e15c329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053327} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dbca48e-b149-5a4e-9fe0-d8e499c7c9d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053322}] :question/id #uuid "421782a5-f8ee-574b-bd35-7a68ca94b6ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-53746" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(0, 1)$ for the curve $x^{2} + xy - y^{2} =-1$."}]}
